  "body": "**What happens.** The `PR-reviewer` checks a PR against the laws (unwrap, commands, layering, placement, process) and runs the touched suites, but nothing tells it *where* a behaviour bug is most likely or what to compare the new code with. With a large diff it ends up sampling. And there is no bound on review rounds: a PR can go `needs-rework` -> fix -> full review -> `needs-rework` again, each round on the same (possibly too cheap) worker tier and each round a full review build.\n\n**Evidence.** In another agent-driven Rust project of this contributor, run with the same builder -> reviewer setup, an evaluation on 2026-11-12 found that reviews without named spots took samples and found nothing; the one real finding of the period came only when the dispatcher named the risky places and the reference function to compare with. The same project caps the loop at one rework per tier.\n\n**Proposal (process files only):**\n1. `work-issue` step 5: the draft PR body carries a `Risk spots:` list - 3-5 entries, each `file:function - what could go wrong - reference` (the issue's Done means, the public source in the module's provenance line, or the existing function / law the change mirrors). Typical spots: new geometry or formulas, anything that reaches posted G-code, file/project writing and round-trip, state across frames, undo/redo and abort paths.\n2. `PR-reviewer` step 5: a new first item \"Risk spots, not samples\": take the PR's list (or choose 3-5 itself when it is missing), read each hunk and its reference, compare step by step, and name in the review what each spot was compared with.\n3. AGENTS.md \"Escalation, never silent\" and `work-issue` step 4: a PR whose second full review is again `needs-rework` is not reworked a third time on the same tier - the issue's effort label rises one step and the next rework goes to that tier (`max`: `blocked`, maintainer decides).\n4. `.claude/PROCESS_VERSION` +1.\n\nSuggested labels: `P5`, `effort:low`, `source:agent` (the contributor cannot set labels).\n\n**Done means:** the four files carry the rules above; a fork that syncs to the new PROCESS_VERSION gets them; #681 has the announcement line when it merges.\n",
